✅ What is Kubernetes?

Kubernetes (K8s) is an open-source container orchestration platform.
It automates deployment, scaling, and management of containerized apps —
especially in production across a cluster of machines.


💡 Why is Kubernetes useful?
- Runs many containers across multiple machines (Nodes)
- Restarts containers/Pods if they fail (self-healing)
- Load balancing + service discovery
- Rolling updates / rollbacks
- Scale up/down (manual or automatic)


🛠️ Real-world use (Spring Boot)
You package the app as a Docker/OCI image. Kubernetes can:
- Run multiple instances (Pods)
- Restart them if they crash
- Spread traffic via a Service / LB
- Scale based on CPU/memory (HPA)


═══════════════════════════════════════
Where do we configure POD count vs CLUSTER size?
═══════════════════════════════════════

Important: these are TWO different places.

  Pod / replica count  →  how many APP copies run
  Node / cluster size  →  how many MACHINES the cluster has


───────────────────────────────────────
A) Pod numbers (replicas) — app level
───────────────────────────────────────

Configured on a Deployment (or StatefulSet), NOT “cluster settings”.

1) In YAML (most common)

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: payment-service
spec:
  replicas: 3                    # ← POD COUNT here
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: payment
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: payment
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: payment
          image: myrepo/payment:1.0
          ports:
            - containerPort: 8080

  replicas: 3  →  keep 3 Pods running

2) Imperative (quick change)

  kubectl scale deployment payment-service --replicas=5

3) Auto — HPA (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler)

═══════════════════════════════════════
What is HPA?
═══════════════════════════════════════

HPA = Horizontal Pod Autoscaler

“Horizontal” = add/remove MORE Pods (scale out / in)
(not “vertical” = give one Pod more CPU/RAM)

Kubernetes watches metrics (usually CPU / memory).
If load is high → create more Pods (up to max).
If load is low  → remove Pods (down to min).

Manual:   you set replicas: 3 and it stays 3
With HPA: replicas move automatically between min and max


Example command:

  kubectl autoscale deployment payment-service --min=2 --max=10 --cpu-percent=70

Meaning:
  min 2     → never fewer than 2 Pods
  max 10    → never more than 10 Pods
  70% CPU   → if average Pod CPU > ~70%, scale UP
              if much lower, scale DOWN


Example HPA YAML:

apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: payment-service-hpa
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: payment-service      # which Deployment to scale
  minReplicas: 2
  maxReplicas: 10
  metrics:
    - type: Resource
      resource:
        name: cpu
        target:
          type: Utilization
          averageUtilization: 70


Dry run (samajhne ke liye):
  Traffic low  → 2 Pods (min)
  Traffic rises, CPU ~80% → 4 Pods, then 6…
  Hits max → stays at 10 (need more Nodes if they don’t fit)
  Traffic drops → slowly back toward 2


HPA vs Cluster Autoscaler:
  HPA                → changes NUMBER OF PODS
  Cluster Autoscaler → changes NUMBER OF NODES (machines)

Need both sometimes:
  HPA wants 10 Pods, but only 2 Nodes with free capacity
  → Cluster Autoscaler adds Nodes so the new Pods can schedule


Requirement:
  Metrics Server (or similar) must be installed
  so HPA can read CPU/memory.


Summary — Pod count lives in:
  Deployment.spec.replicas   (fixed / starting point)
  kubectl scale              (manual change)
  HPA min/max                (automatic change)


───────────────────────────────────────
B) Cluster / Node numbers — infra level
───────────────────────────────────────

This is how many worker machines exist — NOT set in the Deployment YAML.

Examples of where you configure it:

  Managed K8s (EKS / GKE / AKS)
    → Node group / node pool size (desired / min / max nodes)

  kubeadm / on-prem
    → add/remove VMs and join them as Nodes

  Terraform / CloudFormation / pulumi
    → infra-as-code for node count

  Cluster Autoscaler
    → automatically adds/removes Nodes when Pods can’t be scheduled
       or Nodes are underused

Example mental model:

  Cluster has 3 Nodes (infra config)
       └── can schedule many Pods
            └── payment Deployment wants replicas: 3 (app config)

If Pods don’t fit (not enough CPU/RAM on Nodes):
  → need more Nodes (cluster scale), not only higher replicas


───────────────────────────────────────
C) Side-by-side (don’t mix these)
───────────────────────────────────────

What you want              Where you set it
─────────────────────────  ────────────────────────────────────
3 copies of my Spring app  Deployment replicas: 3  (or HPA)
5 worker machines          Cloud node group / Terraform / CA
Scale app with traffic     HPA (Pods)
Scale machines with load   Cluster Autoscaler (Nodes)


Quick commands:

  # Pods for a deployment
  kubectl get deploy payment-service
  kubectl scale deploy payment-service --replicas=4

  # Nodes in the cluster
  kubectl get nodes


═══════════════════════════════════════
Docker vs Kubernetes
═══════════════════════════════════════

Purpose
  Docker      → create, package, run containers
  Kubernetes  → orchestrate many containers across many machines

Level
  Docker      → container / single host (mostly)
  Kubernetes  → cluster level

Use case
  Docker      → one or a few containers on a machine
  Kubernetes  → dozens/hundreds of containers in production

Scaling
  Docker      → manual or Compose/Swarm (limited)
  Kubernetes  → Pod scale (replicas/HPA) + Node scale (cluster)

Networking
  Docker      → basic container networking
  Kubernetes  → Services, DNS, Ingress, cluster networking

Deployment
  Docker      → you run/restart manually or with scripts
  Kubernetes  → rolling updates, self-heal, desired state


💡 In short
Docker builds and runs the container.
Kubernetes decides how many, where, and keeps them healthy across the cluster.

  Pod count   → Deployment / HPA
  Node count  → cloud node pool / Cluster Autoscaler / infra code


🧠 Interview one-liner
“Kubernetes orchestrates containers across a cluster; you set app copies with Deployment replicas (or HPA), and cluster machine count via node groups or Cluster Autoscaler.”
